Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
> Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com> wrote:
>
>> > Is there any real point to classifying and carrying
>> > a bunch of gems around that early in the game?
>
>> Throwing at unicorns to distinguish precious ones from
>> glass, and then to increase your luck by throwing named
>> (non-glass) gems at a co-aligned unicorn.
>
> That would require being fast enough to manage to
> get myself in a straight line with a unicorn.

One of the conundrums of Nethack is you want abilities before they are
easy and by the time they are easy there is little need for them any
more.  Sometimes I count turns to know when I am likely to be able to
toss a gem at a unicorn.  Sometimes I play a class that gets speed at
some XP level.

> I suppose
> if I found early speed boots...  but usually this is very
> much a mid-game option -- by which time I usually[1]
> get lucky enough to find a co-aligned altar, making
> sacrifests an option as well.  I suppose if my dungeon
> did not contain a co-aligned altar, throwing gems to
> unicorns could be a reasonable substitute for getting
> luck up, but again, we're talking mid-game here.

Tossing gems at co-aligned unicorns gives an exact change to your
numerical luck so you can move up doing it.  When your luck is zero
doing a sacrifice increases it a random amount then one per until you
max out.  Given this and how slow a sacrifest can progress I've been
known to max out my luck then kill a co-aligned unicorn to get the exact
numerical luck number for an optimal sac/pray cycle.

>> Then, if you need money,
>
> Need money, in a dungeon that has the gross
> national product plus the national debt laying
> around on the ground in gold coins?  Piles of
> double- and even triple-digit numbers of gold
> coins are literally more common than rocks
> (which, incidentally, gets very annoying when
> I play as a Caveman).  Why on earth would
> I "need money"?

Shops tend to be common before gold is plentiful.  If you have fully
identified valuable gems they are all more efficient at carrying value
than gold.  All players are weight limited most of the game, right?  I
can carry far more value in a few gems than I can in a vast amount of
gold.  At some point in the game they all lose tactical value and I no
longer carry any except to a per-level stash and a single hard gem as an
engraver.

> I've been picking up a couple of thousand coins
> in case I find a shop and leaving the rest until I
> get a bag of holding or locate an aligned priest.
> The last thing I need to do is overtax myself by
> carrying around all that gold, let alone everything
> that can be sold for money.

It makes more sense to loot shops using pets but if you have some
identified gems selling them in trade for shop loot is efficient.

I will point out something else gems are more efficient at - If you are
playing a game where you use a sling, fired gems cause the same amount
of damage as fired rocks but gems weight 1/10th as much as rocks.  It
becomes worthwhile to figure out which gems are glass when you use a
sling.  I have not played many games where I used a sling as my missile
weapon but I have played a few.  Stones limit you by weight much sooner
than pieces of glass.  Heck, late enough in a game and the gems with
lower values end up worth firing at monsters because damage eventually
is worth more than gold.

>> The mentioned camera as well.
>
> I've read about its immense value on the wiki, but
> I've thus far been unable to verify it.  Messing around
> flashing an ineffectual camera at monsters instead
> of shooting them with darts or running away has
> gotten me killed several times, and I've never gotten
> it to actually work to any meaningful extent.

I have similar experiences.

> Usually
> it doesn't scare them, and even if it does they "flee"
> (i.e., briefly stop attacking but continue to remain
> adjacent and chase me if I step away") for 1-2 turns
> and *then* kill me.

Flashed monsters are blind.  Once a monster hits you it no longer
matters if they are blind.  They keep swinging.  The key is to flash and
then move at least one step.  It hardly matters fighting in a corridor
and out in a room it does little to reduce the problem of a crowd. 
Combine these and to me the camera has limited application.  Very nice
when you are faced with a single strong monster but pointless for other
common dangers.

>> [*] Never understood, though, why orbs are so extremely
>> heavy compared to other tools, and they have drawbacks
>> as well; I leave those alone.
>
> I've not experimented much with glass orbs yet, so
> I haven't formed any major opinions about them.

The first time I encountered one I was tempted to carry it.  I quickly
learned how heavy it was and left it in a stash.


